# CARE Frontend

You are an expert in TypeScript, React, Shadcn UI, Tailwind, and CARE frontend development.

This is a healthcare management system for TeleICU & hospital capacity administration.

## Key Principles

- Write concise, technical TypeScript code with accurate examples
- Use functional and declarative programming patterns; avoid classes
- Prefer iteration and modularization over code duplication
- Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., isPatientActive, hasEmergencyStatus)
- Implement HIPAA-compliant data handling practices

## Naming Conventions

- Use lowercase with dashes for directories (e.g., patient-management/, facility-overview/)
- Favor named exports for components
- Use PascalCase for components (PatientOverview, MedicationList)
- Use camelCase for functions and variables (fetchPatientData, isEmergencyCase)

## TypeScript Usage

- Use TypeScript for all code; prefer interfaces over types for medical data structures
- Avoid enums; use maps instead (except for medical constants like BloodGroupChoices)
- Use functional components with TypeScript interfaces
- Strict typing for all medical data and API interfaces

## Syntax and Formatting

- Use the "function" keyword for pure functions
- Avoid unnecessary curly braces in conditionals; use concise syntax for simple statements
- Use declarative JSX
- Handle medical data with proper null checks and error boundaries

## UI and Styling

- Use Shadcn UI, Radix, and Tailwind for components and styling
- Use CAREUI for healthcare-specific components (Calendar, medical forms)
- Implement responsive design with Tailwind CSS; use a mobile-first approach
- Follow medical color conventions (red=critical, yellow=warning, green=stable)

## Healthcare Data & API Guidelines

- CARE uses TanStack Query for data fetching with query and mutate utilities (src/Utils/request/README.md)
- APIs are defined in *.Api.ts files under src/types/ directories
- Use zod validation for all medical data inputs
- Implement audit logging for medical data access
- Handle PHI (Protected Health Information) with appropriate security measures

## Routing & Navigation

- Use raviger for healthcare workflow routing
- Organize routes by medical domains (Patient/, Facility/, Medication/)
- Implement proper navigation history for clinical workflows

## Internationalization

- Add and reuse proper translations in en.json for medical terminology
- Use consistent medical term translations across components
- Support healthcare-specific date/time formatting

## Healthcare Standards

- HIPAA compliance and PHI protection in all data handling
- WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility for medical environments
- Sub-100ms response times for emergency workflows
- Proper error boundaries for critical medical data

## Testing Guidelines

For Cypress testing guidelines, refer to cypress/docs/*.md for healthcare workflow testing patterns.

## Reference

See .github/copilot-instructions.md and .cursor/rules/*.mdc for comprehensive guidance.
